How search engines work 

Search engines are special sites on the web that are designed to help people find information stored on other sites. Here are a few examples- Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Yahoo, Ecosia, Ask, Yandex and Baidu. There are various ways in which search engines work, i.e. small differences from one search engine to another, but they all perform three basic tasks- 
Firstly, they search the internet or select pieces of the internet for information through a process called crawling.
Next, this information is analysed and stored in a large database through a process called indexing.
Lastly, when a user searches for something, the search engine scans its index to find the most relevant results through a process called ranking.

